166	- `FND-0195` [corroborated; {'Tate_range': 'n-1<=c(g) for published c(3)=c(4)=11,c(5)=7,c(6)=5,c(7)=3', 'campaign_case': {'g': 6, 'k': 26, 'n': 6}, 'coefficients': 'Q', 'degree': 'k=vcd(M_{g,n})=4g-4+n', 'excludes': ['degree vcd-1', 'unpublished c(8)'], 'g': '>=2', 'n': '>=1', 'object': 'lowest-weight ordinary cohomology of the open moduli stack M_{g,n}'}]: Let g>=2, n>=1, and work with rational coefficients on Deligne-Mumford stacks. Write C^n for the n-fold fibre product of the closed universal curve pi:C->M_g, and set k=vcd(M_{g,n})=4g-4+n. The open immersion M_{g,n} subset C^n induces a surjection W_k H^k(C^n;Q) ->> W_k H^k(M_{g,n};Q) by Deligne's identification of the lowest-weight piece in degree k. Canning-Larson-Payne Lemma 3.1(a) then supplies an isomorphism of pure weight-k Hodge structures W_k H^k(M_{g,n};Q)/(Phi_{g,n}^k+Psi_{g,n}^k) congruent to the direct sum over |lambda|=n of W_k H^{k-n}(M_g;V_lambda) tensor V_{lambda^T}, where Phi_{g,n}^k=sum_i pi_i^* W_k H^k(M_{g,n-1};Q) and Psi_{g,n}^k=sum_i psi_i Phi_{g,n}^{k-2}; the identification of Psi with the Kunneth summands having some i_s=2, modulo Phi, is the published pi_2-projector statement of that lemma (Petersen-Tavakol-Yin), not a fibrewise canonical-class slogan. Harer's virtual cohomological dimension gives H^p(M_g;L)=0 for every Q-local system L and every p>vcd(M_g)=4g-5 (equivalently: cohomological dimension of a torsion-free finite-index subgroup, transferred by rational Hochschild-Serre). Hence every multi-index i in {0,1,2}^n with |i|<=n has base degree k-|i|>=4g-4>4g-5 and the primitive quotient vanishes. Independently k=vcd(M_{g,n-1})+1, so Phi_{g,n}^k=0. Therefore W_{vcd} H^{vcd}(M_{g,n};Q)=Psi_{g,n}^{vcd}=sum_i psi_i sum_j pi_j^* W_{vcd-2} H^{vcd-2}(M_{g,n-1};Q). The comparison vcd(M_{g,n})-n=4g-4=vcd(M_g)+1 is independent of n>=1. Each psi_i is algebraic of type Q(-1). Whenever n-1<=c(g) in the published CKgP table c(3)=c(4)=11, c(5)=7, c(6)=5, c(7)=3, Canning-Larson-Payne Proposition 4.5 implies that W_{vcd-2} H^{vcd-2}(M_{g,n-1};Q) is algebraic of Tate type, and semisimplicity of polarizable pure Q-Hodge structures of a single weight realises W_{vcd} H^{vcd}(M_{g,n};Q) as a finite direct sum of Q(-vcd/2). In particular, at the campaign pair (g,n)=(6,6) one has k=26, n-1=5=c(6), and W_26 H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q) is a finite direct sum of Q(-13). The same peel does not kill the primitive term in degree vcd-1 and does not apply where no published c(g) is available.
167	- `FND-0196` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}, 'coefficients': 'Q', 'g': '>=2', 'n': '>=1', 'object': 'n-fold fibre product of the closed universal curve over M_g'}]: Work over Q with g>=2. Let pi:C->M_g be the universal closed curve and C^n its n-fold fibre product. Write omega_s=pr_s^* c_1(omega_{C/M_g}); on C this is the class psi_1 of Petersen-Tavakol-Yin, and its restriction to the open configuration space M_{g,n} subset C^n is the tautological psi class. The Leray-Kunneth decomposition of the smooth proper map C^n->M_g splits H^*(C^n) into summands H^{k-|i|}(M_g; tensor_s R^{i_s} pi_* Q) indexed by i in {0,1,2}^n. The class omega_s is not homogeneous for this multi-index: it decomposes as a_s+b_s+c_s with a_s in H^2(M_g) (slot value 0), b_s in H^1(M_g; R^1 pi_* Q) (slot value 1), and c_s in H^0(M_g; R^2 pi_* Q) (slot value 2). The fibre restriction of omega_s is the canonical class of degree 2g-2, so c_s equals 2g-2 times the generator of R^2 pi_* Q isomorphic to Q(-1). Only c_s induces the isomorphism from the i_s=0 summand onto the i_s=2 summand; cup-product with a_s preserves the multi-index while raising base degree by 2, and cup-product with b_s raises the s-th slot by 1. Consequently any argument of the form 'modulo summands with some slot 0, multiplication by psi_s (or omega_s) spans the i_s=2 summands because the class restricts to a nonzero multiple of the fibre canonical class' must dispose of the slot-1 cross term b_s. Three disposals are valid over Q. (1) The slot-1 summand vanishes: H^1(M_g; R^1 pi_* Q) isomorphic to H^1(Mod_g; H^1(Sigma_g; Q))=0 for g>=2. This is the rational form of Morita's theorem H_1(Mod_g; H_1(Sigma_g; Z)) isomorphic to Z/(2g-2) for g>=2, recorded as H^1(Gamma_g, H_Q)=0 by Hain-Reed; equivalently H^2(C; Q) has basis {lambda, omega}, so the H^1(M_g; V) summand of H^2(C) is zero, hence b_s=0 identically and modulo slot-0 summands multiplication by omega_s coincides with multiplication by c_s. (2) The relative Chow-