168	- `FND-0197` [corroborated; {'campaign_case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}, 'coefficients': 'Q', 'excludes': ['identification of ker of restriction on W_k', 'Gysin exactness for diagonal divisors', 'symplectic Schur-Weyl or traceless isomorphism', 'Kunneth summands of H^*(C^n) as summands of R^* f_* Q for f:M_{g,n}->M_g'], 'g': '>=2', 'ground_field': 'C', 'n': '>=2', 'objects': ['n-fold fibre product C^n of the closed universal curve over M_g', 'open complement of pairwise diagonals identified with M_{g,n}'], 'ordinary_cohomology': 'W_k H^k'}]: Work over C with Q-coefficients. Let g>=2 and n>=2. Let pi:C->M_g be the universal closed curve of genus g, and let C^n be its n-fold fibre product over M_g, a smooth separated finite-type Deligne-Mumford stack. Identify M_{g,n} with the open complement in C^n of the union of pairwise diagonals. Then, for every integer k, restriction along this open immersion is a morphism of mixed Hodge structures inducing a surjection W_k H^k(C^n;Q) ->> W_k H^k(M_{g,n};Q). The surjection follows from Deligne, Theorie de Hodge II, Corollaire 3.2.17: for a smooth separated scheme (and, with Q-coefficients, a smooth finite-type Deligne-Mumford stack via a scheme atlas), W_k H^k equals the image of H^k of any smooth proper compactification; a smooth proper compactification of C^n is simultaneously a compactification of the open M_{g,n}, so both lowest-weight groups equal the image of the same compactification cohomology and restriction factors that image. Consequently W_k H^k(M_{g,n};Q) is a quotient of W_k H^k(C^n;Q), and any generation, vanishing, or Tate-type upper bound on the source yields the same upper bound on the target. Identifying the kernel of restriction on W_k, including Gysin exactness for the diagonal divisors and the symplectic Schur-Weyl or traceless decomposition of (R^1 pi_* Q)^{tensor n}, is needed only to upgrade the surjection to an isomorphism (Canning-Larson-Payne Lemma 3.1(a)). This surjection does not assert that Kunneth summ