nces are smooth of pure dimension n_j with affine coarse space. Then n_0=dim Y^(0)=dim Y for every admissible chain, so the top index is chain-independent even though, by FND-0192(4), residual strata of dimensions 9 through 16 remain chain-dependent. Three consequences for C66-TATE-SUPPORT, using FND-0192(3)-(4) and Artin vanishing H^k(A;Q)=0 only for k>dim A on a possibly singular complex affine variety (Esnault, Theorem 2.1; FND-0192 (S4)). (a) If dim Y<=8, then every n_j<=8. Graded pieces with n_j<=7 vanish and the at most one n_j=8 piece is a finite direct sum of Q(8), possibly zero, so T_Z is a finite direct sum of Q(8) and the assigned theorem is discharged with no work in dimensions 9 through 16. This is strictly stronger than FND-0186, which gives K(Y')=0 only for dim Y'<8 via the compact-support bound 16>2 dim Y'; at dim Y=8 the filtration yields Tate type rather than vanishing. (b) If 9<=dim Y<=16, then n=dim Y occurs as a residual index for every admissible chain, so no choice of chain removes the obligation at that index. Lower residual indices in {9,...,16} minus {dim Y} remain chain-dependent: dim Y pins the top residual index, not the full residual set. (c) If dim Y>=17, the top piece vanishes by Artin vanishing after rational coarse comparison, because the source degree 2 n_0-16 exceeds n_0. Remaining residual indices form a chain-dependent subset of {9,...,16}; dim Y does not enumerate them. Presently no verified artifact computes dim Y: ATT-0120 and FND-0192 assume no dimension of Y; FND-0181 proves only dim Y<=20; FND-0187 records that citing CLM-0112-8 unchanged does not license a PGL_2-quotient repair dim Y<=17 and proves no equality. One therefore cannot yet decide which of (a), (b), (c) holds. Independently of dim Y, FND-0192 already lists the ambient residual range as a subset of {9,...,16}, so that range is enumerable. Determining dim Y is a prerequisite for deciding whether the residual range collapses as in (a) or which top index is forced as in (b), but is not a prerequisite for bounding residual work to those eight dimensions. Not asserted: a value of dim Y; chain-independence of residual strata other than n_0; present discharge of C66-TATE-SUPPORT.
- `FND-0195` [corroborated; {'Tate_range': 'n-1<=c(g) for published c(3)=c(4)=11,c(5)=7,c(6)=5,c(7)=3', 'campaign_case': {'g': 6, 'k': 26, 'n': 6}, 'coefficients': 'Q', 'degree': 'k=vcd(M_{g,n})=4g-4+n', 'excludes': ['degree vcd-1', 'unpublished c(8)'], 'g': '>=2', 'n': '>=1', 'object': 'lowest-weight ordinary cohomology of the open moduli stack M_{g,n}'}]: Let g>=2, n>=1, and work with rational coefficients on Deligne-Mumford stacks. Write C^n for the n-fold fibre product of the closed universal curve pi:C->M_g, and set k=vcd(M_{g,n})=4g-4+n. The open immersion M_{g,n} subset C^n induces a surjection W_k H^k(C^n;Q) ->> W_k H^k(M_{g,n};Q) by Deligne's identification of the lowest-weight piece in degree k. Canning-Larson-Payne Lemma 3.1(a) then supplies an isomorphism of pure weight-k Hodge structures W_k H^k(M_{g,n};Q)/(Phi_{g,n}^k+Psi_{g,n}^k) congruent to the direct sum over |lambda|=n of W_k H^{k-n}(M_g;V_lambda) tensor V_{lambda^T}, where Phi_{g,n}^k=sum_i pi_i^* W_k H^k(M_{g,n-1};Q) and Psi_{g,n}^k=sum_i psi_i Phi_{g,n}^{k-2}; the identification of Psi with the Kunneth summands having some i_s=2, modulo Phi, is the published pi_2-projector statement of that lemma (Petersen-Tavakol-Yin), not a fibrewise canonical-class slogan. Harer's virtual cohomological dimension gives H^p(M_g;L)=0 for every Q-local system L and every p>vcd(M_g)=4g-5 (equivalently: cohomological dimension of a torsion-free finite-index subgroup, transferred by rational Hochschild-Serre). Hence every multi-index i in {0,1,2}^n with |i|<=n has base degree k-|i|>=4g-4>4g-5 and the primitive quotient vanishes. Independently k=vcd(M_{g,n-1})+1, so Phi_{g,n}^k=0. Therefore W_{vcd} H^{vcd}(M_{g,n};Q)=Psi_{g,n}^{vcd}=sum_i psi_i sum_j pi_j^* W_{vcd-2} H^{vcd-2}(M_{g,n-1};Q). The comparison vcd(M_{g,n})-n=4g-4=vcd(M_g)+1 is independent of n>=1. Each psi_i is algebraic of type Q(-1). Whenever n-1<=c(g) in the published CKgP table c(3)=c(4)=11, c(5)=7, c(6)=5, c(7)=3, Canning-Larson-Payne Proposition 4.5 implies that W_{vcd-2} H^{vcd-2}(M_{g,n-1};Q) is algebraic of Tate type, and semisimplicity of polarizable pure Q-Hodge structures of a single weight realises W_{vcd} H^{vcd}(M_{g,n};Q) as a finite direct sum of Q(-vcd/2). In particular, at the campaign pair (g,n)=(6,6) one has k=26, n-1=5=c(6), and W_26 H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q) is a finite direct sum of Q(-13). The same peel does not kill the primitive term in degree vcd-1 and does not apply where no published c(g) is available.
- `FND-0196` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}, 'coefficients': 'Q', 'g': '>=2', 'n': '>=1', 'object': 'n-fold fibre product of the closed universal curve over M_g'}]: Work over Q with g>=2. Let pi:C->M_g be the universal closed curve and C^n its n-fold fibre product. Write omega_s=pr_s^* c_1(omega_{C/M_g}); on C this is the class psi_1 of Petersen-Tavakol-Yin, and its restriction to the open configuration space M_{g,n} subset C^n is the tautological psi class. The Leray-Kunneth decomposition of the smooth proper map C^n->M_g splits H^*(C^n) into summands H^{k-|i|}(M_g; tensor_s R^{i_s} pi_* Q) indexed by i in {0,1,2}^n. The class omega_s is not homogeneous for this multi-index: it decomposes as a_s+b_s+c_s with a_s in H^2(M_g) (slot value 0), b_s in H^1(M_g; R^1 pi_* Q) (slot value 1), and c_s in H^0(M_g; R^2 pi_* Q) (slot value 2). The fibre restriction of omega_s is the canonical class of degree 2g-2, so c_s equals 2g-2 times the generator of R^2 pi_* Q isomorphic to Q(-1). Only c_s induces the isomorphism from the i_s=0 summand onto the i_s=2 summand; cup-product with a_s preserves the multi-index while raising base degree by 2, and cup-product with b_s raises the s-th slot by 1. Consequently any argument of the form 'modulo summands with some slot 0, multiplication by psi_s (or omega_s) spans the i_s=2 summands because the class restricts to a nonzero multiple of the fibre canonical class' must dispose of the slot-1 cross term b_s. Three disposals are valid over Q. (1) The slot-1 summand vanishes: H^1(M_g; R^1 pi_* Q) isomorphic to H^1(Mod_g; H^1(Sigma_g; Q))=0 for g>=2. This is the rational form of Morita's theorem H_1(Mod_g; H_1(Sigma_g; Z)) isomorphic to Z/(2g-2) for g>=2, recorded as H^1(Gamma_g, H_Q)=0 by Hain-Reed; equivalently H^2(C; Q) has basis {lambda, omega}, so the H^1(M_g; V) summand of H^2(C) is zero, hence b_s=0 identically and modulo slot-0 summands multiplication by omega_s coincides with multiplication by c_s. (2) The relative Chow-Kunneth projector of Petersen-Tavakol-Yin, pi_2 = psi_2/(2g-2) - kappa_1/(2(2g-2)^2), already lies in the Q-span of a psi class and a pullback from M_g, so it realises the i_s=0 to i_s=2 isomorphism inside Psi+Phi; Canning-Larson-Payne Lemma 3.1 invokes these projectors. (3) Downward induction on |i|: writing A_i for the multi-index summand of W_k H^k(C^n) and B_{i'} for the class with slot s reset to 0, one has A_i = c_s B_{i'} inside omega_s B_{i'} + A_{i'} + A_{i''} with |i''|=|i|-1, using the base case A_j=0 whenever k-|j| exceeds vcd(M_g)=4g-5. Disposal (3) applies in particular at the campaign point (g,n,k)=(6,6,26), where every multi-index with |i|<=6 has base degree at least 20>19. Thus the containment of i_s=2 summands in Phi+Psi holds over Q, but a fibre-restriction sentence does not by itself justify it.
- `FND-0197` [corroborated; {'campaign_case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}, 'coefficients': 'Q', 'excludes': ['identification of ker of restriction on W_k', 'Gysin exactness for diagonal divisors', 'symplectic Schur-Weyl or traceless isomorphism', 'Kunneth summands of H^*(C^n) as summands of R^* f_* Q for f:M_{g,n}->M_g'], 'g': '>=2', 'ground_field': 'C', 'n': '>=2', 'objects': ['n-fold fibre product C^n of the closed universal curve over M_g', 'open complement of pairwise diagonals identified with M_{g,n}'], 'ordinary_cohomology': 'W_k H^k'}]: Work over C with Q-coefficients. Let g>=2 and n>=2. Let pi:C->M_g be the universal closed curve of genus g, and let C^n be its n-fold fibre product over M_g, a smooth separated finite-type Deligne-Mumford stack. Identify M_{g,n} with the open complement in C^n of the union of pairwise diagonals. Then, for every integer k, restriction along this open immersion is a morphism of mixed Hodge structures inducing a surjection W_k H^k(C^n;Q) ->> W_k H^k(M_{g,n};Q). The surjection follows from Deligne, Theorie de Hodge II, Corollaire 3.2.17: for a smooth separated scheme (and, with Q-coefficients, a smooth finite-type Deligne-Mumford stack via a scheme atlas), W_k H^k equals the image of H^k of any smooth proper compactification; a smooth proper compactification of C^n is simultaneously a compactification of the open M_{g,n}, so both lowest-weight groups equal the image of the same compactification cohomology and restriction factors that image. Consequently W_k H^k(M_{g,n};Q) is a quotient of W_k H^k(C^n;Q), and any generation, vanishing, or Tate-type upper bound on the source yields the same upper bound on the target. Identifying the kernel of restriction on W_k, including Gysin exactness for the diagonal divisors and the symplectic Schur-Weyl or traceless decomposition of (R^1 pi_* Q)^{tensor n}, is needed only to upgrade the surjection to an isomorphism (Canning-Larson-Payne Lemma 3.1(a)). This surjection does not assert that Kunneth summands of H^*(C^n) are summands of R^* f_* Q for the non-proper map f:M_{g,n}->M_g; that identification remains invalid, because the fibres are configuration spaces of distinct points and R^1 of the punctured curve is mixed of weights 1 and 2. In particular the statement applies at the campaign case (g,n,k)=(6,6,26).
