  "strongest_attack": "Binding first: TASK.json and ATT-0141 agree on packet_sha256 a898256aaf8511a3d1cb2fcd8b257b2280656f698053d628ac21d697ef9bd191, packet_binding_sha256 6a765b58376ddfd3446271493007689a0f0aa98ecd4931dc4ae2eabb6610db54, the full 13-key target dictionary (g=5, n=8, dim 20, open_bm_degree 16 / Q(8) / weight -16, ordinary degree 24 / Q(-12) / weight 24, poincare_twist 20, chow_codim 12), and the theorem_statement string; the artifact under review is the one the task names. Strongest attack attempted: DEPTH/SCOPE. The lemma is stated for one-edge graphs only, so I attacked the restriction by asking whether RED-0001 pairs re-enter at codimension >= 2, which is how the subproblem's own informal wording ('the boundary of Mbar_{5,8} lies outside RED-0001', packet L23) would be read. This attack SUCCEEDS against the loose wording and FAILS against the stated theorem: the stable graph with a single genus-3 vertex carrying all eight labelled legs and two loops has b_1=2, total genus 3+2=5, inclusive marking count 8+4=12, is stable (2*3-2+12=16>0), and has dim Mbar_{3,12}=18=20-2, so Mbar_{3,12} really does occur as the vertex atlas of a codimension-two boundary stratum of Mbar_{5,8}. The one-edge hypothesis is therefore load-bearing and sharp, not cosmetic. It is legitimate here because THM-0003's hat{partial} is the normalization of the boundary DIVISOR, i.e. the disjoint union over one-edge graphs of [Mbar_Gamma/Aut(Gamma)], which is exactly the convention the packet fixes for the parallel (6,6) case at L12-L14. I also checked the dual question and found the exclusion is even stronger for the other pairs: no boundary stratum of Mbar_{5,8} of any codimension has a genus-5 vertex with 8 inclusive markings, because a genus-5 vertex forces a tree with all other vertices rational, each edge at v consumes at least two legs into its rational subtree, giving n_v <= (8-2E_v)+E_v = 8-E_v <= 7; and genus >= 6 vertices are impossible at any depth. So (3,12) is the uniq