      "statement": "The RED-0001 residual condition 'required ordinary degree 2d-16 equals vcd' is equivalent to the closed form 2g + n = 18: 2(3g-3+n) - 16 = 4g-4+n reduces to 2g+n = 18, reproducing exactly the pairs (3,12), (5,8), (6,6), (7,4), (8,2). Equivalently n-1 = 17-2g. The published CKgP table used by FND-0195 satisfies c(g) = 17-2g precisely for g = 5, 6, 7, and c(3) = 11 = 17-6. Hence for every RED-0001 residual pair with a published c(g), the FND-0195 hypothesis n-1 <= c(g) is met at equality with zero slack, never with margin: (3,12), (5,8), (6,6) and (7,4) all sit exactly on the endpoint, while (8,2) is unreachable only because c(8) is unpublished. Two consequences. (a) These four discharges share a single correlated point of failure: any narrowing of the published CKgP hypothesis to a strict inequality, or any downward correction of one c(g), invalidates the corresponding case outright with no fallback inside the peel, since every other vanishing in the peel carries strict slack of exactly one (k > vcd(M_{g,n-1}) and k-n > vcd(M_g)). (b) The alignment is not a coincidence of the campaign's case list: g = 4 gives n = 10 with n-1 = 9 < c(4) = 11, the unique slack case, and (4,10) is correspondingly absent from the residual list. Reviewers should therefore treat 'the endpoint is inclusive' as a shared load-bearing assumption across these cases rather than as a per-attempt detail, and should not read confirmation of one endpoint case as independent evidence for another.",
